---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="School readiness" description="How Mini Brains Trust play helps get your 3 to 5 year old ready for their first year of school, mapped to the Australian Curriculum. Readiness, not replacement.">
  <main>
    <header class="srhero">
      <span class="blob" style="width:300px;height:300px;background:var(--pink);opacity:.5;right:-70px;top:-20px;"></span>
      <span class="blob b2" style="width:210px;height:210px;background:var(--peri);opacity:.3;left:-50px;top:60px;"></span>
      <span class="blob b3" style="width:170px;height:170px;background:var(--yellow);opacity:.55;left:120px;bottom:-30px;"></span>
      <div class="wrap center">
        <h1>Getting ready for school, one playful day at a time.</h1>
        <p class="lead">Mini Brains Trust is not a school, and it does not try to be. But we know what children are expected to do when they walk into their first classroom, because we have mapped our play against the Australian Curriculum. Every activity is designed to grow the human strengths and early foundations that give your child a confident start.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="bridge">
          <h2>The readiness years come first</h2>
          <p class="lede">In Australia, the curriculum formally begins at Foundation, the first year of school, called Prep, Kindergarten, Reception or Pre-Primary depending on your state. Mini Brains is made for the 3 to 5s, the readiness years just before. We build the ground that school then builds on: the language, the number sense, the curiosity, the focus and the confidence a child brings with them on day one.</p>
          <div class="flow">
            <div class="step"><div class="age">Ages 3 to 5</div><div class="lbl">Mini Brains Trust: playful readiness at home</div></div>
            <div class="arw">&rarr;</div>
            <div class="step school"><div class="age">Foundation</div><div class="lbl">First year of school: the curriculum begins</div></div>
            <div class="arw">&rarr;</div>
            <div class="step school"><div class="age">Year 1 and on</div><div class="lbl">Learning builds on strong foundations</div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="wrap center">
        <h2>What Foundation expects, and how our play helps.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The left of each card is drawn from ACARA's own plain-language guide for parents. The right is how Mini Brains play quietly builds toward it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="areas">

          <article class="area a-eng">
            <div class="ahead"><span class="dot"></span><h3>English</h3><span class="tag">Literacy foundations</span></div>
            <div class="cols">
              <div class="col expect"><div class="lab">What Foundation expects</div>
                <ul>
                  <li>Enjoy picture books and rhymes, and communicate in familiar situations</li>
                  <li>Retell stories and personal experiences</li>
                  <li>Recognise rhyming words, syllables and sounds</li>
                  <li>Name letters and the most common sounds they make</li>
                  <li>Create texts, out loud and in writing</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="col help"><div class="lab">How Mini Brains helps</div>
                <p>Every activity is talked through together, so children practise describing, explaining and retelling out loud, the roots of oral language. Rhyme, syllable and sound play grows phonological awareness, the strongest early predictor of reading. Story-led themes invite children to narrate and imagine.</p>
                <div class="themes"><span class="t">Story</span><span class="t">Sounds</span><span class="t">Birds</span></div>
                <div class="grows">Grows: Connection, Curiosity</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="area a-math">
            <div class="ahead"><span class="dot"></span><h3>Mathematics</h3><span class="tag">Numeracy foundations</span></div>
            <div class="cols">
              <div class="col expect"><div class="lab">What Foundation expects</div>
                <ul>
                  <li>A sense of shape, size, number, order, pattern and position</li>
                  <li>Count and connect numbers and quantities to 20</li>
                  <li>Recognise repeating patterns</li>
                  <li>Compare, group, sort and name familiar shapes</li>
                  <li>Use location words like above, below and next to</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="col help"><div class="lab">How Mini Brains helps</div>
                <p>Children count, match, compare and sort through hands-on play, so number stays real, not abstract. They spot and extend patterns, name shapes as they build, and use position words as they pack, tidy and arrange. Maths lives in the kitchen and the toy box, exactly where it should start.</p>
                <div class="themes"><span class="t">Numbers</span><span class="t">Shapes</span><span class="t">Patterns</span><span class="t">Boxes</span></div>
                <div class="grows">Grows: Attention, Sticking at it</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="area a-sci">
            <div class="ahead"><span class="dot"></span><h3>Science</h3><span class="tag">Curiosity and observation</span></div>
            <div class="cols">
              <div class="col expect"><div class="lab">What Foundation expects</div>
                <ul>
                  <li>Build wonder and curiosity about everyday things</li>
                  <li>Observe the features of plants and animals</li>
                  <li>Investigate the properties of everyday materials</li>
                  <li>Explore how things move</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="col help"><div class="lab">How Mini Brains helps</div>
                <p>So many of our themes are simply looking closely and having a go: watching a bird, testing what floats, noticing a shadow move. Children predict, try, and notice what happened, the exact habits of a young scientist, long before they meet the word.</p>
                <div class="themes"><span class="t">Minibeasts</span><span class="t">Water</span><span class="t">Seeds</span><span class="t">Magnets</span><span class="t">Shadows</span><span class="t">Weather</span></div>
                <div class="grows">Grows: Curiosity, Real world</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="area a-hass">
            <div class="ahead"><span class="dot"></span><h3>Humanities and Social Sciences</h3><span class="tag">People and place</span></div>
            <div class="cols">
              <div class="col expect"><div class="lab">What Foundation expects</div>
                <ul>
                  <li>Explore their personal world and family history</li>
                  <li>Investigate places they live in and belong to</li>
                  <li>Find out about other places through stories</li>
                  <li>Explore why places are special and how to care for them</li>
                  <li>Recognise the importance of Country and Place to First Nations Australians</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="col help"><div class="lab">How Mini Brains helps</div>
                <p>Play and conversation about family, home and the places we go build early history and geography through noticing and talking. Real-world settings invite children to wonder about other places and to care for their own, the first steps of a curious, connected citizen.</p>
                <div class="themes"><span class="t">Beach</span><span class="t">Shop</span><span class="t">Kitchen</span><span class="t">Living</span></div>
                <div class="grows">Grows: Connection, Real world</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="area a-hpe">
            <div class="ahead"><span class="dot"></span><h3>Health and Physical Education</h3><span class="tag">Body, feelings and relationships</span></div>
            <div class="cols">
              <div class="col expect"><div class="lab">What Foundation expects</div>
                <ul>
                  <li>Practise movement skills and learn about the body</li>
                  <li>Name and describe emotions: happy, sad, excited, tired, angry, scared, confused</li>
                  <li>Ask permission when sharing space or possessions</li>
                  <li>Show actions that ensure fair play</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="col help"><div class="lab">How Mini Brains helps</div>
                <p>Our Feelings theme names and explores emotions, the exact readiness skill, while Body and Move build coordination and body awareness. Because play is shared and turn-taking, children practise asking, sharing and fair play. The Rescue button gently models calming a big feeling.</p>
                <div class="themes"><span class="t">Feelings</span><span class="t">Body</span><span class="t">Move</span><span class="t">Balance</span></div>
                <div class="grows">Grows: Connection, Agency</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="area a-arts">
            <div class="ahead"><span class="dot"></span><h3>The Arts</h3><span class="tag">Creative play</span></div>
            <div class="cols">
              <div class="col expect"><div class="lab">What Foundation expects</div>
                <ul>
                  <li>Use purposeful, creative play to make and share arts works</li>
                  <li>Explore sounds by listening, singing and making music</li>
                  <li>Respond to and create artworks by drawing and painting</li>
                  <li>Use imagination in dance and drama</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="col help"><div class="lab">How Mini Brains helps</div>
                <p>Open-ended making, drawing and building run through our themes, and Colours and Mixing turn art into discovery. Sound and song play explore music, and imaginative, dramatic play is woven in. Children create something and share it, the heart of the Arts.</p>
                <div class="themes"><span class="t">Colours</span><span class="t">Mixing</span><span class="t">Sounds</span></div>
                <div class="grows">Grows: Creativity, Agency</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="area a-tech">
            <div class="ahead"><span class="dot"></span><h3>Technologies</h3><span class="tag">Design and everyday tech</span></div>
            <div class="cols">
              <div class="col expect"><div class="lab">What Foundation expects</div>
                <ul>
                  <li>Discover how technologies work through exploration, design and guided play</li>
                  <li>Follow steps to safely make a designed solution</li>
                  <li>Become familiar with how people use digital systems</li>
                  <li>Represent data as objects, pictures and symbols</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="col help"><div class="lab">How Mini Brains helps</div>
                <p>Making activities follow simple steps to build something, the seed of design. Sorting and representing objects, pictures and symbols lays early groundwork for data and thinking. And the whole app is built on the curriculum's own design-thinking process, which you can read about below.</p>
                <div class="themes"><span class="t">Boxes</span><span class="t">Shapes</span><span class="t">Patterns</span></div>
                <div class="grows">Grows: Real world, Sticking at it</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

          <article class="area a-lang">
            <div class="ahead"><span class="dot"></span><h3>Languages</h3><span class="tag">Listening and culture</span></div>
            <div class="cols">
              <div class="col expect"><div class="lab">What Foundation expects</div>
                <ul>
                  <li>Learn a language other than English through play and imagination</li>
                  <li>Imitate sounds, gestures and expressions</li>
                  <li>Listen to songs, picture books and stories</li>
                  <li>Appreciate that language and culture are related</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="col help"><div class="lab">How Mini Brains helps</div>
                <p>We are not a languages app, and we will not pretend to be. But the listening, sound play and cultural curiosity our activities build are the ground that language learning rests on: an ear tuned to sounds, and an openness to how other people see the world.</p>
                <div class="themes"><span class="t">Sounds</span><span class="t">Story</span></div>
                <div class="grows">Grows: Curiosity, Connection</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="wrap center">
        <h2>Our eight strengths are the curriculum's capabilities.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Beyond the subjects, the Australian Curriculum names seven general capabilities: the dispositions every child needs to learn, live and work. The human strengths Mini Brains grows are those same capabilities, starting early.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="caps">
          <div class="caprow"><span class="mbt">Curiosity and Creativity</span><span class="to">&rarr;</span><span class="cap">Critical and Creative Thinking</span></div>
          <div class="caprow"><span class="mbt">Connection and Agency</span><span class="to">&rarr;</span><span class="cap">Personal and Social capability</span></div>
          <div class="caprow"><span class="mbt">Attention and Sticking at it</span><span class="to">&rarr;</span><span class="cap">The self-regulation that underpins Literacy and Numeracy</span></div>
          <div class="caprow"><span class="mbt">Real world</span><span class="to">&rarr;</span><span class="cap">Ethical and Intercultural Understanding: fairness, care and curiosity about others</span></div>
          <div class="caprow"><span class="mbt">Discernment</span><span class="to">&rarr;</span><span class="cap">Early Digital Literacy and critical thinking about the world</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="panel lav">
          <h2>Built on the curriculum's own design thinking.</h2>
          <p class="lede">Design thinking is a core concept of the Australian Curriculum: Technologies. It is how young designers learn to solve real problems: Understand, Ideate, Model, Reflect. Mini Brains is built on that same loop, and our play invites children into it, noticing a problem, trying an idea, having a go, and thinking about what happened. So the way your child plays mirrors the way the curriculum teaches thinking.</p>
          <div class="dtsteps"><span>Understand</span><span>Ideate</span><span>Model</span><span>Reflect</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="fineprint">
          <h3>The honest fine print</h3>
          <p>Mini Brains Trust supports school readiness. It does not replace school or formally teach the curriculum, which begins at Foundation. We build the foundations and dispositions a child brings with them.</p>
          <p>The expectations on the left of each card are drawn from the Australian Curriculum V9 (Foundation) and ACARA's parent and carer information. Mini Brains Trust is independent and is not affiliated with, or endorsed by, ACARA.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="final" id="join">
      <div class="wrap center">
        <h2>Give your little one a confident start.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Join the waitlist for early access, a founding-member price, and a real say in what we build.</p>
        <form class="form" id="waitlist-form" name="waitlist" method="POST" data-netlify="true" netlify-honeypot="bot-field">
          <input type="hidden" name="form-name" value="waitlist" />
          <p hidden><label>Leave empty: <input name="bot-field" /></label></p>
          <div class="row">
            <input type="text" name="first_name" placeholder="Your first name" autocomplete="given-name" />
            <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="Your email" required autocomplete="email" />
          </div>
          <button type="submit" class="btn lg">Join the waitlist &nbsp;&rarr;</button>
        </form>
        <div class="thanks" id="thanks">
          <div class="fred">You're on the list.</div>
          <p>Thanks for backing this early. We'll be in touch before anyone else.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .srhero{position:relative;overflow:hidden;padding:26px 0 8px;}
  .srhero .wrap{position:relative;z-index:1;padding-top:16px;padding-bottom:8px;}
  .srhero h1{font-size:clamp(32px,5vw,52px);max-width:20ch;margin:0 auto 18px;}
  .srhero .lead{font-size:clamp(16px,1.5vw,18.5px);color:var(--grey);max-width:60ch;margin:0 auto;}

  .bridge{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:24px;padding:28px 30px;box-shadow:0 12px 32px rgba(44,44,52,.06);}
  .bridge h2{font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,28px);margin-bottom:8px;}
  .flow{display:flex;align-items:stretch;gap:10px;margin-top:22px;flex-wrap:wrap;}
  .flow .step{flex:1;min-width:160px;background:var(--berrytint);border-radius:16px;padding:16px 18px;}
  .flow .step.school{background:var(--cream);border:1px dashed var(--line);}
  .flow .step .age{font-family:'Fredoka';font-weight:700;color:var(--berryink);font-size:15px;}
  .flow .step .lbl{font-size:14px;color:var(--grey);margin-top:4px;line-height:1.4;}
  .flow .arw{align-self:center;color:#c8c2cc;font-size:24px;font-family:'Fredoka';}

  .areas{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;margin-top:8px;}
  .area{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:22px;box-shadow:0 12px 30px rgba(44,44,52,.06);overflow:hidden;--c:var(--berry);}
  .ahead{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;padding:16px 26px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);border-top:4px solid var(--c);}
  .area .dot{width:13px;height:13px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--c);flex:none;}
  .ahead h3{font-size:20px;color:var(--ink);margin:0;}
  .ahead .tag{font-size:13px;color:var(--grey);margin-left:auto;font-weight:600;font-family:'Fredoka';}
  .cols{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;}
  .col{padding:20px 26px 22px;}
  .col + .col{border-left:1px solid var(--line);}
  .lab{font-family:'Fredoka';font-weight:600;font-size:11.5px;letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:11px;}
  .col.expect .lab{color:var(--grey);}
  .col.help .lab{color:var(--c);}
  .col ul{margin:0;padding-left:18px;}
  .col li{margin:7px 0;font-size:15px;color:var(--grey);line-height:1.5;}
  .col.help p{margin:0 0 12px;font-size:15px;color:var(--grey);line-height:1.6;}
  .themes{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;}
  .themes .t{font-family:'Fredoka';font-weight:600;font-size:12px;color:#fff;background:var(--c);padding:3px 11px;border-radius:100px;opacity:.92;}
  .grows{margin-top:13px;font-size:13px;color:var(--grey);font-family:'Fredoka';font-weight:500;}

  .a-eng{--c:#E85D93;} .a-math{--c:#6F97E8;} .a-sci{--c:#4AA85C;} .a-hass{--c:#E0824A;}
  .a-hpe{--c:#C56AAE;} .a-arts{--c:#8B7BD8;} .a-tech{--c:#2F9E98;} .a-lang{--c:#C08A1E;}

  .caps{max-width:760px;margin:0 auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:22px;padding:6px 28px;box-shadow:0 12px 30px rgba(44,44,52,.06);}
  .caprow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto 1.1fr;gap:16px;align-items:center;padding:15px 0;border-bottom:1px dashed var(--line);}
  .caprow:last-child{border-bottom:0;}
  .caprow .mbt{font-family:'Fredoka';font-weight:600;color:var(--berryink);font-size:15.5px;}
  .caprow .to{color:#c8c2cc;font-size:20px;text-align:center;}
  .caprow .cap{font-size:15px;color:var(--grey);}

  .dtsteps{display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:18px;}
  .dtsteps span{font-family:'Fredoka';font-weight:600;font-size:13.5px;color:var(--berryink);background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);padding:7px 15px;border-radius:100px;}

  .fineprint{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:18px;padding:22px 26px;max-width:820px;margin:0 auto;}
  .fineprint h3{font-family:'Fredoka';font-weight:600;font-size:16px;color:var(--ink);margin-bottom:8px;}
  .fineprint p{font-size:14px;color:var(--grey);margin-top:8px;line-height:1.6;}

  @media(max-width:680px){
    .cols{grid-template-columns:1fr;}
    .col + .col{border-left:0;border-top:1px solid var(--line);}
    .caprow{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:6px;text-align:center;}
    .caprow .to{transform:rotate(90deg);}
  }
</style>
